A||||7||85ZainoGT||tinting windows yourself?||||1||
Z||000000||85ZainoGT||10-01-2001||04:58 PM||||originally I was gonna have someone put my pre-cut tint on for me, but is it really that hard to tint your windows yourself?  i ordered the kit from fiero fanaticus... what do you think?||||reg||1
Z||000001||BlackARROW||10-01-2001||05:13 PM||||It's not that hard, but for the rear window. A spare set of hands would be of a great help. The key is too much soapy/water is not enough.. It will give you plenty of time to place it properly.  Use the little rubber scrapper, and that's it.  Application between 15 and 20°C in a shaddy environment is perfect (don't know °F, sorry  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Good luck<BR><P>------------------<BR>BlackARROW &gt;&gt;--&gt;<BR>Gadgeteered Fiero 2M4 '85<BR>"Get your groove on"||||reg||1
Z||000002||Voytek||10-01-2001||05:56 PM||||Quite easy, but a spare set of hands on the back window is definitely an asset.<P>To do the back window properly, you should remove the side plastic panels, and possibly the 3rd brake light, if you have one.<P>Tip: the 'carpetty' stuff bellow the back window is a pain for little 'hairs'.  Tape it off completely with some masking tape - it'll save you a lot of cursing.<P>Just to be safe, you should also think about taping off the roof panel just around the window.<P>PLENTY, PLENTY of soap water!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||86seyellow||10-01-2001||07:08 PM||||I did all mine, 2 sides and the rear myself. Took me about 1hour for all. GOt to use lots and lots of liquid so you odnt get wrinkles. I used the darkewst tint I could find to match the trim with my yellow. You cant see in at all.<P>------------------<BR>I can go as fast as I want unless I get caught<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieropix1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||85ZainoGT||10-01-2001||11:11 PM||||well I did my two side windows, looks pimp so far, one tiny bubble on passenger side but its in the bottom corner so not bad for a virgin tinter.. im happy with it, just got the back one to do..||||reg||1
Z||000005||BlackARROW||10-02-2001||05:12 PM||||One thing I did to remove small bubbles is to take an hexacto (cutter) to make tiny tiny incision to remove the water/air from there, and stick that plastic right on.  <P>I say, remove that before hot and cold days.  Just imagine being the poor little bubble, where would you go having a lighter or icecube up your a..?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Good job - Good luck for the rear one!<P><P>------------------<BR>BlackARROW &gt;&gt;--&gt;<BR>Gadgeteered Fiero 2M4 '85<BR>"Get your groove on"||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroFanaticus2||10-02-2001||05:40 PM||||Take a hair dryer and blow hot air on the outside of the window over the air bubble.  While doing that press the bubble against the window.  After a few minutes of doing that the bubble should stick to the window.<BR>This works great for those little 'finger runs' too.||||reg||1
Z||000007||85ZainoGT||10-02-2001||09:32 PM||||ok, ill try what you are suggesting, thanks...||||reg||1
